    "statement": "The sequence 30,55,91,140,204,285,385,506,650,819,1015,1240,1496,1785,2109,2470,2870,3311 for x0=4..21. Differences: 25,36,49,64,81,100,121,144,169,196,225,256,289,324,361,400,441. These are (k+1)^2 for k=4..20, i.e., perfect squares starting at 25=5^2. So f(n) = f(4) + sum_{k=5}^{n+1} k^2 = 30 + sum_{k=5}^{n+1} k^2. Actually f(n) - f(n-1) = (n+1)^2. So f(n) = sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k^2 - (1+4+9+16) + 30 = sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k^2 - 30 + 30 = sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k^2. Check: sum_{k=1}^{5} k^2 = 1+4+9+16+25=55. But f(4)=30, f(5)=55. So f(n) = sum_{k=1}^{n+1} k^2 doesn't work for n=4. Let me try: f(n) = n(n+1)(2n+1)/6. For n=4: 4*5*9/6=30. Yes! f(5)=5*6*11/6=55. f(6)=6*7*13/6=91. Perfect! This is the sum of squares formula."
